WEBVTT

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:05.630
 Delicious Revolution is brought to you by us, by me and Chelsea, specifically by our consulting business.

00:00:05.630 --> 00:00:11.520
 We provide participatory research, communications, and creative work to organizations that are building food movements.

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:14.370
 What's your food movement? Let us know and get in touch.

00:00:14.370 --> 00:00:18.540
 DeliciousRevolutionShow.com

00:00:18.540 --> 00:00:33.220
 One of the most frequent questions I hear is, "But Anna, can we really feed the world using organic farming practices or using sustainable practices?"

00:00:33.220 --> 00:00:41.460
 And increasingly, there is scientific evidence that shows that farming solutions that help farmers get rid of their chemicals,

00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:49.220
 those are the solutions that not only create great productivity at the farm level, but they're also economically great for farmers.

00:00:49.220 --> 00:00:54.100
 It's huge. I mean, it's the myth my mom has been trying to debunk for four decades.

00:00:54.100 --> 00:00:59.140
 But the reason why it is so hard to debunk isn't because the evidence is on our side.

00:00:59.140 --> 00:01:07.380
 It's because there is such a profit to be made in the status quo. There are such interests aligned with having us

00:01:07.380 --> 00:01:15.380
 believe that we need this chemical arsenal in order to feed the world, that we need to be using these genetically engineered seeds to feed the world,

00:01:15.380 --> 00:01:21.380
 that we need synthetic fertilizer to feed the world. You're talking about billions and billions of dollars at stake.

00:01:21.380 --> 00:01:32.700
 Welcome to Delicious Revolution, a show about food, culture, and place. I'm Chelsea Wills.

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:40.870
 And I'm Devin Sampson. We talk with people who work in and think about all aspects of food, including farmers, chefs, artists, and activists,

00:01:40.870 --> 00:01:44.390
 bringing you in-depth conversations with some amazing people.

00:01:44.390 --> 00:01:51.030
 On this third season of Delicious Revolution, we're bringing you stories and perspectives from the unseen places in food systems,

00:01:51.590 --> 00:02:00.390
 going behind kitchen doors, into underground nests of native bees, under the waves, and to the far away origins of flavors we love, just to name a few.

00:02:00.670 --> 00:02:09.090
 I'm speaking with people who work with food in places we normally cannot see or don't notice. It's a season of unseen stories of food.

00:02:09.090 --> 00:02:17.810
 You can subscribe to Delicious Revolution on iTunes or your favorite podcast app. Just search for Delicious Revolution wherever you get your podcasts and you'll find us.

00:02:17.810 --> 00:02:24.930
 Our website is deliciousrevolutionshow.com, where we have pictures, links, and more information about all the people on our show.

00:02:24.930 --> 00:02:39.150
 Anna LePay is a best-selling author and widely respected educator, known for her work as an expert on food systems and as a sustainable food advocate.

00:02:39.150 --> 00:02:45.440
 Anna is a founding principal of the Small Planet Institute and the Small Planet Fund with her mother, Frances Moore-Bloppe.

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:58.530
 She is also the founder and director of the Real Food Media Project, which uses creative movies, an online movie contest, a web-based action center, and grassroots events to grow the movement for sustainable food and farming.

00:02:58.970 --> 00:03:08.150
 Her latest book, Diet for a Hot Planet, The Climate Crisis at the End of Your Fork and What You Can Do About It, was named by Booklist and Kirkus as one of the best environmental books of the year.

00:03:08.550 --> 00:03:17.750
 Anna was also the author of Hope's Edge, which chronicles social movements fighting hunger around the world, and Grubb, Ideas for an Urban Organic Kitchen, with Chef Brian Terry.

00:03:18.220 --> 00:03:20.820
 Here's Chelsea's interview with Anna LePay.

00:03:22.050 --> 00:03:26.720
 When did your work with food start, and how did that love come about?

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:30.980
 I think the origin story for me really starts in my childhood.

00:03:31.400 --> 00:03:53.220
 My mother is Frances Moore-Loppe, who wrote when she was 26 years old, wrote a book called Diet for a Small Planet that set her on her life's course of following her questions around why we have hunger in the world, and why do we have hunger in a world when there's plenty of food to feed us all, which was true when she wrote her book almost 40 years ago, and it's still true today.

00:03:53.220 --> 00:04:05.240
 And so from a really early age, I had a household and a family that was asking these questions about food that was really the center of our family was really the dinner table.

00:04:05.500 --> 00:04:22.030
 And so it was really a part of my life growing up, but as an adult, this path that I'm on really started when I was a graduate student, and I was studying economic and political development and burying my head in books and textbooks.

00:04:22.030 --> 00:04:35.960
 And I was really frustrated by the limitations of what I was learning in the classroom, and I encouraged my mother to revisit those questions she was asking in Diet for a Small Planet and write another book.

00:04:35.960 --> 00:04:42.650
 And she'd written many books in the interim, but to really revisit Diet for a Small Planet with a 30th anniversary edition.

00:04:43.090 --> 00:05:04.450
 And she agreed, she thought it was wise counsel from her daughter, and I ended up working with her initially just as a research assistant, and then as the work progressed as a co-author on a book that took us on a journey from India to Bangladesh to Poland, Kenya, France, and several other countries, as well as places within the United States.

00:05:04.450 --> 00:05:16.390
 And that experience is really what set me on what's been now 15-year life path of being an advocate for sustainable food systems, being an advocate for justice along the food chain.

00:05:16.650 --> 00:05:20.130
 And it was really that journey that set me on this path.

00:05:20.860 --> 00:05:21.020
 Cool.

00:05:21.500 --> 00:05:32.860
 Something that kind of strikes me when I hear you talk about that is that it sounds like you work with a lot of different aspects of the food system, and that's always been part of your work.

00:05:32.860 --> 00:05:36.000
 So you don't just work with farmers or chefs.

00:05:36.160 --> 00:05:40.330
 You kind of do a lot of the bridging the gaps between places and people.

00:05:40.430 --> 00:05:40.870
 Is that true?

00:05:41.650 --> 00:05:42.050
 That's right.

00:05:42.270 --> 00:05:47.590
 I like to think of myself as a generalist, although I dove into specific topics in this work.

00:05:47.590 --> 00:06:06.770
 Yes, I very much see my particular role that I can play is providing those connections, making those connections, helping people see that food is not just an environmental issue, but a social justice issue, and not just in a social justice issue, but also a cultural, spiritual issue.

00:06:07.150 --> 00:06:24.180
 So drawing those connections has been always really important to me, and also really trying to help specialists in particular fields really amplify their message and their story so that their messages can be understood by a really wide audience.

00:06:24.180 --> 00:06:30.810
 Could you give me – I think that's such an important role, that kind of synthetic role in movement, right?

00:06:30.910 --> 00:06:51.390
 It creates this context that I think is so necessary for people to really be able to do their jobs as farmers or as – I'm thinking about the edible schoolyard people reinventing kind of what school lunches look like or something like that.

00:06:51.470 --> 00:06:55.790
 I think that these bigger containers really support that.

00:06:55.790 --> 00:07:01.350
 Can you give me an example of how you do that, or what did that look like maybe on your trip?

00:07:01.800 --> 00:07:19.130
 Yeah, well, I think a really great example of that is the last book I wrote called Diet for a Hot Planet, obviously an homage to my mother's book, Diet for a Small Planet, that was really helping draw those connections between the food system and climate disruption.

00:07:19.130 --> 00:07:42.360
 And I think that book really brings to life this attempt that I was making to draw those connections and draw communities together, so to really help environmentalists and those really focused on addressing the climate crisis, help them understand how the food system, the global food system, is both a driver of the crisis as well as really a key part of the solution.

00:07:42.360 --> 00:08:01.440
 And to really understand and to really understand and bring farmers into the story of how we're going to fix climate change by helping people see that when farmers are using the most sustainable methods, they're actually able to reduce the amount of energy used in a farming operation, reduce the greenhouse gas emissions from a farming operation.

00:08:01.440 --> 00:08:12.360
 And even better than that, help store carbon in the soils so that farms can actually be part of what a climate scientist would call a mitigation strategy.

00:08:13.200 --> 00:08:29.970
 And my book was really an attempt to help people draw those connections at a moment when the book came out several years ago that the story of food and climate was really not a core part of the story about climate change and how we're going to address it and take it on.

00:08:29.970 --> 00:08:45.590
 And when I started working on Diet for Hot Planet, I was really struck by analysis that colleagues of mine had done at Johns Hopkins University that looked at all of the newspaper articles in the U.S. from 2006 to 2008 about climate change.

00:08:45.590 --> 00:09:14.120
 And they did an analysis to look at how many of them talked about food systems or agriculture, and it was less than 2% of those articles about climate change mentioned food systems or agriculture, despite the fact that our global food system is responsible for about one-third of all the greenhouse gas emissions that's causing the crisis, largely because agribusiness is one of the biggest forces behind forest destruction,

00:09:14.120 --> 00:09:20.320
 which we know is a huge, huge problem in terms of global warming and climate disruption.

00:09:20.880 --> 00:09:31.320
 So that, to me, was evidence that there was a conversation gap, and I really wanted to help bridge that gap by helping spark those conversations.

00:09:33.040 --> 00:09:43.250
 Well, I just saw you talk in February in Petaluma, and I was really struck by how that conversation has changed since your book came out.

00:09:43.250 --> 00:09:45.030
 And I feel like there's so much more of a link.

00:09:46.020 --> 00:09:48.300
 Do you feel like that's true?

00:09:48.940 --> 00:09:49.700
 Oh, absolutely.

00:09:49.980 --> 00:10:00.120
 And by no means, don't get me wrong, I do not take credit for the fact that I do feel like there has been this shift in the past five years, let's say.

00:10:00.560 --> 00:10:04.320
 I do not take personal credit, but I think there's been a shift, and there's a couple reasons why.

00:10:04.360 --> 00:10:15.270
 First of all, I think that myself and many other advocates have really pushed a lot of the big environmental groups to see the importance of talking about food systems,

00:10:15.410 --> 00:10:23.270
 to see the importance about taking on agribusiness as one of the corporate drivers of global warming.

00:10:23.270 --> 00:10:35.400
 I think, secondly, the climate movement as a whole is just much more sophisticated, and really, we know so much more now than we did five years ago.

00:10:36.060 --> 00:10:52.950
 And the third thing is I think those of us who might consider ourselves more squarely in the food movement are ourselves understanding the need to not silo ourselves and not think about food as separate from the environment, as separate from climate, but to really see them together.

00:10:53.030 --> 00:11:10.310
 And then the final thing I would say is that I think all of us are so much clearer that the impacts of climate change aren't going to happen in some distant, imagined future, that we are experiencing the impacts in a major way today.

00:11:10.710 --> 00:11:18.150
 And I would argue that farmers, those who are working on the ground, in the ground, in the soil, are on the front lines of that impact.

00:11:18.150 --> 00:11:36.040
 So there's a much deeper understanding that farmers are among the key casualties of climate change, and therefore figuring out how to ensure that farmers are able to stay on their land, farm sustainably, and be protected from the climate disruption is a really, really critical part of the conversation.

00:11:36.040 --> 00:11:55.490
 Well, it just brought up that change, because I think that's a pretty extraordinary change in discourse over the last 10 years, I would say, but that so much coalition building has happened, so much coming together has happened with the climate movement in a way that I felt like was unimaginable before.

00:11:55.890 --> 00:11:57.350
 So I feel like that's really hopeful.

00:11:57.350 --> 00:12:26.390
 Well, I think it is, I would also caution us to add to that, or balance it with saying, there's, I think when you look at what the conversation is in the halls of Congress, or, you know, sort of the international treaty level, you know, still food systems and agribusiness role in rainforest destruction is still certainly not at the heart of those conversations as much as it needs to be.

00:12:26.390 --> 00:12:39.750
 Well, absolutely, I think there's, I mean, I also feel, unfortunately, concerned that, that there's still so much lack of awareness on these issues that people are easily influenced by misinformation.

00:12:40.310 --> 00:13:00.110
 And one of the examples of that, I was just listening to an interview with the filmmaker behind a movie called Cowspiracy, which puts out totally wrong facts and figures about these issues, and says and claims that livestock alone is responsible for half of all of greenhouse gas emissions.

00:13:00.110 --> 00:13:02.290
 And that's just patently untrue.

00:13:02.290 --> 00:13:16.330
 And ultimately, it's so frustrating because the film is speaking to people who care about these issues, whose hearts and minds are very much in sync with many of the groups that I work with.

00:13:16.670 --> 00:13:20.050
 And yet what they're getting in the film is this absolute misinformation.

00:13:20.950 --> 00:13:24.310
 And I think ultimately, it's a really divisive film.

00:13:24.590 --> 00:13:32.870
 And, and so that, you know, so I would just say, I would caution us to say that there's still so much education work to be done to inoculate people from misinformation from both sides.

00:13:33.530 --> 00:13:38.250
 And Cowspiracy, I think, is a great example of some of that misinformation that's getting out there.

00:13:38.910 --> 00:13:47.610
 Well, that was what I was going to ask you next with the changing conversation that I think allows for more of that complexity to come to the surface, right?

00:13:47.610 --> 00:13:55.890
 I think there is maybe this basic awareness that climate change and food politics are connected to each other, right?

00:13:56.090 --> 00:14:00.850
 But, but now what, what's really important for people to know about what's happening now?

00:14:01.050 --> 00:14:02.000
 Right, right.

00:14:02.100 --> 00:14:12.520
 So, you know, I think on, on this topic, I think what's really important for people to know about is, um, is people to understand kind of food systems on these multiple levels.

00:14:12.520 --> 00:14:19.960
 I imagine them as kind of concentric circles moving out from us as an individual to our community, to our country, to the world.

00:14:19.960 --> 00:14:37.460
 Um, and in that smallest concentric circle of oneself, uh, I really like to stress that there are some basic practices we can all deploy when it comes to our own, uh, consumption patterns and our own relationship to food that put us squarely aligned with a climate friendly diet.

00:14:37.460 --> 00:14:52.260
 And those are things that probably many people who might be listening to your podcast are already doing things like supporting organic farmers who by design, by the organic practices are much, uh, uh, are much better for the environment and the climate.

00:14:52.260 --> 00:14:58.480
 By doing things like reducing the amount of processed foods we eat and, and packaged foods we eat.

00:14:58.480 --> 00:15:10.040
 And if we are, uh, typical, uh, American eating a typical amount of meat and consuming the typical amount of dairy in this country, we could definitely reduce that meat and dairy consumption.

00:15:10.040 --> 00:15:20.090
 Because the lion's share of meat and dairy produced in this country is still done in such, um, horribly polluting and, uh, and really, really energy intensive ways.

00:15:20.210 --> 00:15:28.010
 Um, so there's these really basic principles we can, we can uphold as individuals, but then as we move out to these broader concentric circles, what can be done?

00:15:28.010 --> 00:15:49.690
 And I'm really encouraged by the work of advocates who are working on policies at the state level here in California and then policies on the national level that are really looking at how can we put money behind the practices that are going to improve, uh, agriculture, reduce the greenhouse gas emissions from the sector.

00:15:49.690 --> 00:15:59.170
 And, uh, uh, have other kinds of co-benefits like not creating terribly polluting, uh, factory farms that are impacting waterways and impacting the air.

00:15:59.170 --> 00:16:02.990
 And there's great policies here in California that are doing that, that we can get behind.

00:16:02.990 --> 00:16:12.970
 Uh, and there's work, uh, on the national level to ensure things like, um, our conservation, uh, uh, programs at the national level support that kind of farming.

00:16:12.970 --> 00:16:21.450
 And then internationally, there is still so much work to be done to really halt the incredibly destructive practices of agribusiness companies.

00:16:21.830 --> 00:16:38.370
 Groups, uh, that I'm involved with include Rainforest Action Network that's doing some of that work that's, you know, direct action, directly taking on those corporate players who are really at the heart of, of the climate crisis and need to be called out as much as we call out Exxon and Shell and BP.

00:16:38.370 --> 00:16:52.790
 Um, so, so we, so we, we need to work at all these levels and of course not every single one of us can or should work on all those levels, but it's important to really understand that, that those multiple levels exist and, and, and, and all of them need to be actively engaged.

00:16:52.790 --> 00:16:57.830
 Maybe let's back up and let's talk a little bit more specifically about each one of those levels.

00:16:58.490 --> 00:17:03.990
 So, um, in the basic practices levels, you're talking about supporting organic farmers.

00:17:04.390 --> 00:17:14.170
 So in a fairly literal way, that means like if you live somewhere where there's a farmer's market or something like that, that's a good place to shop or what, I mean, what can people actually do?

00:17:14.610 --> 00:17:14.810
 Yeah.

00:17:14.910 --> 00:17:18.530
 So it's seeking out organic and, and, and local products.

00:17:18.530 --> 00:17:30.350
 I mean, I'm so fortunate that I live in a place, uh, in the East Bay and the San Francisco Bay area in California, where that's, uh, relatively easy for me.

00:17:30.350 --> 00:17:44.130
 That is not the case for many of us, but for those of us who do live in places that can seek out, uh, locally owned and operated grocery stores or cooperatively owned grocery stores that are really supporting, uh, the local farming base.

00:17:44.130 --> 00:17:46.290
 We can put our dollars in that direction.

00:17:46.290 --> 00:17:55.290
 We can do things like, uh, uh, shop at a farmer's market, which most farmer's markets in this country require that if you're selling food there, you are also a producer.

00:17:55.290 --> 00:18:01.350
 So buying in a farmer's market means that you're really investing in your local food economy.

00:18:01.830 --> 00:18:17.260
 And we can, of course, also seek out that USDA organic seal, uh, and, and in doing so again, uh, when you support those kinds of food production practices, it means you're supporting farmers that aren't using petroleum based chemicals.

00:18:17.480 --> 00:18:25.600
 That are really building the soil fertility, which means increasing the soil carbon, taking that carbon dioxide in the atmosphere.

00:18:25.600 --> 00:18:29.740
 That's a contributor, a big contributor to climate change and storing it in the soil.

00:18:29.920 --> 00:18:33.880
 That is one of the outcomes of good organic farming practices.

00:18:34.660 --> 00:18:50.200
 And while I said, most of us, not all of us live in places where seeking out these foods is easy, increasingly thanks to advocacy and activism, it's, it's, uh, more possible than it's been ever before.

00:18:50.200 --> 00:18:59.080
 I think about farmer's markets, for instance, in the 1970s, when I was born, there were several hundred farmer's markets left in this country.

00:18:59.080 --> 00:19:10.800
 Today, the USDA Agricultural Marketing Service tracks the official number of farmers markets inched up past 8,400 this year.

00:19:10.960 --> 00:19:17.660
 So we're really talking about thousands of communities around the country where you can have that direct connection.

00:19:17.660 --> 00:19:32.370
 We also can support our local farmers through subscription services like community-supported agriculture, where you can have a direct relationship with the farm and get produce or other products from, from that farm or from a collection of farms.

00:19:32.470 --> 00:19:38.920
 So those are all great, great ways to support, uh, your local economy and, and have food that, again, it's not just climate friendly.

00:19:38.920 --> 00:19:44.140
 It's also fresher, uh, healthier, uh, and again, great for your local economy.

00:19:46.000 --> 00:19:51.490
 And what about at this mid-level, what does that look like for people who would like to get involved?

00:19:52.520 --> 00:20:05.690
 Well, you know, for people who are really passionate about getting involved at that policy level and that advocacy level, uh, it's really seeking out the groups in your state that are working at that state level.

00:20:05.690 --> 00:20:20.950
 And, uh, and, you know, it can be as small step as, uh, getting on those groups' email lists and picking up the phone when they say, hey, call your representative, let them know you, you know, you stand for, for, for this policy that, that we're pushing for at the state level.

00:20:20.950 --> 00:20:25.700
 You know, or it can mean really thinking about, might it be a career path for you?

00:20:25.760 --> 00:20:27.660
 Might you want to get into policy work?

00:20:27.740 --> 00:20:34.260
 We need so many more people with the values of sustainability and justice working for policy change.

00:20:34.420 --> 00:20:41.420
 Here in California, there's a great group called CALCAN, which stands for the California Climate and Agriculture Network.

00:20:41.820 --> 00:20:51.400
 And they've really been at the heart of helping to, to conceive and draft policies that would really benefit farmers in the state as well as benefit the climate.

00:20:51.680 --> 00:20:53.180
 And there's lots of groups.

00:20:53.280 --> 00:20:56.340
 Every single state has groups that are working, uh, at that state level.

00:20:56.800 --> 00:21:03.420
 And, again, it sort of depends on, on how much you want to throw your hat in the ring, but I think there's a lot of opportunity there that we could, we could tap into.

00:21:03.980 --> 00:21:04.790
 Wonderful.

00:21:05.110 --> 00:21:15.160
 And, and at a global level, I remember hearing the reports back from Paris in November and feeling struck at how much was about food this time.

00:21:15.460 --> 00:21:17.210
 So could you kind of recap that?

00:21:17.530 --> 00:21:19.770
 Yeah, again, you know, so much more.

00:21:19.890 --> 00:21:21.870
 And I would argue we still have so much farther to go.

00:21:21.870 --> 00:21:30.230
 I think, you know, I'll, I don't think, um, many people would disagree with me there that there's still so much more work to be done to bring the conversation forward.

00:21:30.470 --> 00:21:45.660
 And, you know, I think that the conversation at that international level and how much it's changed, I think really speaks to the advocacy of, of the work of grassroots groups around the world,

00:21:46.080 --> 00:21:55.830
 not letting their leaders be silent on this issue that is, one could argue, one of the most important issues that our planet faces.

00:21:56.390 --> 00:22:05.350
 I remember marching in the streets of New York City for the People's Climate March and being struck that it was hundreds of thousands of people that turned out for that march.

00:22:05.570 --> 00:22:09.070
 And there were, uh, solidarity marches all around the world.

00:22:09.070 --> 00:22:19.640
 And I, I think that we need all forms of advocacy and activism to really make the change that's needed in the world.

00:22:19.640 --> 00:22:24.480
 And, and one of those forms of activism is, is getting out in the streets and making your voices heard.

00:22:24.480 --> 00:22:30.430
 And it was so encouraging to hear so many people, uh, in the streets and see so many people.

00:22:30.430 --> 00:22:32.690
 And again, to see food being a part of that.

00:22:32.770 --> 00:22:39.350
 So the Organic Consumers Association here in the U.S. organized a section of that march for food activists.

00:22:39.350 --> 00:22:53.070
 And, uh, I remember standing in the midst of all of these hundreds of hundreds, thousands of people with their, um, their signs promoting that link between organic food and a cool planet.

00:22:53.070 --> 00:22:54.910
 And it was really encouraging to see that.

00:22:56.030 --> 00:22:57.030
 Yeah, that's really wonderful.

00:22:57.030 --> 00:23:06.830
 I think it's so nice to hear about all those different levels because, um, you know, part of the magic of food is that we, we have to engage with it multiple times a day.

00:23:06.830 --> 00:23:07.230
 Right.

00:23:07.290 --> 00:23:09.250
 But sometimes it feels like you're all by yourself.

00:23:09.710 --> 00:23:17.600
 So it's nice to hear about, um, ways that it brings kind of these disparate groups together or seemingly disparate groups.

00:23:18.000 --> 00:23:18.480
 Absolutely.

00:23:18.620 --> 00:23:19.300
 It really can.

00:23:19.300 --> 00:23:33.220
 And I, I often think about a, uh, statistic from the Department of Agriculture and about how much of our food dollar goes to farmers on average, uh, the typical food dollar that's spent.

00:23:33.380 --> 00:23:47.980
 And, uh, the Department of Agriculture recalibrates this figure, uh, every few years, but it's roughly about 10 to 20 cents of every dollar we spend is actually going to farmer, the actual producer.

00:23:47.980 --> 00:23:51.400
 And so much of it is going to marketing, to packaging, to transportation.

00:23:51.860 --> 00:24:11.770
 And again, when I think about how it makes me feel so much more connected to my food, to be seeking out ways to directly support producers, whether it's through a community supported agriculture program or through a farmer's market or through buying from a local store that I know has great practices with its purchases.

00:24:12.350 --> 00:24:16.490
 Uh, you know, those are things that, that, that add to that feeling of connection and connectivity.

00:24:16.770 --> 00:24:18.170
 Yeah, absolutely.

00:24:18.170 --> 00:24:21.590
 So let's back up and get a little more abstract.

00:24:21.790 --> 00:24:25.570
 What, what do you imagine a healthy food system to look like?

00:24:29.910 --> 00:24:37.500
 Well, I think a healthy food system is healthy for every element in that system.

00:24:37.500 --> 00:25:08.110
 And that, and that's why I think this word system is so important because you can't have a healthy system if, if, uh, the, the workers, the farm workers, the, uh, truck drivers who are transporting the food, the, the processors who are processing it aren't themselves healthy or, uh, given, uh, a workplace where they're given real dignity or have protections on the job from work.

00:25:08.130 --> 00:25:13.050
 So, uh, similarly, you can't have a healthy food system if, uh, you know, the workers are protected and the food is good for the eater.

00:25:13.050 --> 00:25:21.670
 Uh, uh, the climate is, uh, the climate is despoiled in the process of producing that food.

00:25:21.830 --> 00:25:31.630
 And, and, and similarly, you can't have a healthy food system if, you know, so yes, the eaters might be healthy and the climate might, and the environment might be protected and the workers might be protected.

00:25:31.630 --> 00:25:41.270
 But if you're exploiting, uh, animals in the practice of getting that food to your plate, if, if animals are treated inhumanely, similarly, that's not a healthy food system.

00:25:41.690 --> 00:25:46.490
 And, and so, you know, I like to think of them as all interlocking parts.

00:25:46.990 --> 00:26:00.120
 And, uh, what is encouraging is that so often when you get each of those pieces in the system, right, it has a ripple effect that improves other parts of the system.

00:26:00.120 --> 00:26:07.500
 And so, for instance, a lot of the work I do is with, uh, food worker organizations, um, who work along the food chain.

00:26:07.840 --> 00:26:21.520
 And one of the arguments I make is if you look at what is good, for instance, for a, a worker in a meat processing plant, what's good for that worker on the floor of that meat processing plant is a slower, a slower line.

00:26:21.520 --> 00:26:29.900
 So they're not having to repeat the same practices at a, such a speed that ultimately is totally damaging to their bodies.

00:26:30.240 --> 00:26:44.720
 Uh, what's also, you know, good for them, for that worker is a plant that is handling its waste in a way that protects the worker from, uh, uh, fumes or off gases, uh, that protects the workers, uh, in, in those ways.

00:26:44.800 --> 00:26:51.350
 So I could go down the line with all of these, these practices that would need to change in a meat processing plant that would be good for a worker.

00:26:51.690 --> 00:27:01.290
 And almost every single one of those practices are also going to increase the level of, uh, of animal welfare that you see in that operation.

00:27:01.290 --> 00:27:10.550
 If you slow down line speeds, if you're handling the waste better, if you're, um, improving those practices for workers, it also has that improvement for the, for animal welfare as well.

00:27:10.550 --> 00:27:21.730
 As well as for, for, for, for the human health that it decreases the amount of, uh, the likelihood of a foodborne illness, for instance, uh, that could be impacting a consumer at the end of the chain.

00:27:21.730 --> 00:27:35.090
 So, um, so, so, so, so often what's great is that when you start looking at food as a system and solution systemically, they have a ripple effect and a co-benefit across the solutions.

00:27:35.290 --> 00:27:45.120
 Yeah. And I think, you know, there's been, I'm thinking about around 2008 when there was this whole vote with your fork kind of idea.

00:27:45.540 --> 00:27:56.640
 And I think what quickly happened with that idea was that people realized that because of these interlocking systems, there was no way to just vote with your fork as a consumer, right?

00:27:56.780 --> 00:27:59.390
 All these things got complicated.

00:27:59.390 --> 00:28:27.100
 Right. And I, I think this is where I, I get concerned that sometimes my message gets, uh, misconstrued in the game of telephone that is sometimes, uh, our, our media, which is that I think that we are, are not going to fix our, our broken food system through acting solely as consumers.

00:28:27.100 --> 00:28:32.020
 And, and only as you say, voting with our fork, there's a lot of reasons why that's the case.

00:28:32.020 --> 00:28:51.680
 I think one of them is that for that it, there are so many consumers, if that's your frame that we need to vote with your fork, there are so many consumers that I would describe as then disenfranchised eaters that they cannot vote with their fork because a, they don't have access to the kind of food that they would want to support with their food dollars.

00:28:51.680 --> 00:29:06.980
 It's not in their communities. It's not in their grocery stores. Um, secondly, you have a lot of consumers who, who don't have the, um, who, who don't have the income to be making different choices about what, what they're spending, uh, food on.

00:29:06.980 --> 00:29:15.050
 So you're talking about if you're only limited, if you're only saying to people, Hey, how you can make change in the system is by what you purchase.

00:29:15.050 --> 00:29:25.130
 You're shutting out millions of people from, from being part of the change and you're really limiting, uh, your understanding of how change happens.

00:29:25.130 --> 00:29:38.410
 It doesn't just happen in a marketplace. We need advocates that are, uh, are pushing corporations in, uh, really powerful ways to change their supply chain.

00:29:38.410 --> 00:29:41.390
 It doesn't just happen at the cash register at the grocery store.

00:29:41.510 --> 00:29:50.970
 Yeah. Thanks. Thanks for saying that. Cause I think, I think one of the big criticisms of the food movement is, um, oftentimes that it's really elitist, right?

00:29:50.970 --> 00:30:07.730
 And that it does exclude these huge swaths of people and, and the world. Right. So, well, and I think that that's, to me, I, I always find myself getting, uh, frustrated when I hear that depiction of the food movement, because that isn't the food movement that I know.

00:30:07.730 --> 00:30:20.760
 It's not the food movement that I see or work with. Uh, I think some of that, you know, not to say that that isn't, there isn't some truth to that, but I feel like that is, uh, another example of how the media has mischaracterized

00:30:20.760 --> 00:30:33.610
 What I think, um, this broader food movement as I see it stands for. Because when I see and think about the groups that are part of my definition of the food movement, um, it's so much broader than that.

00:30:33.610 --> 00:30:39.030
 And I see the food movement as, as really, uh, human, part of the human rights movement and part of the environmental movement.

00:30:39.030 --> 00:30:52.110
 And I see some of the best leaders in the food movement are those representing farm workers, those representing, uh, uh, uh, communities that are, uh, uh, are not getting access to, to healthy food.

00:30:52.190 --> 00:31:02.140
 You know, I don't, I don't, I don't, I don't think of, you know, that wealthy, uh, whole foods grocery shopper as synonymous with the food movement.

00:31:02.140 --> 00:31:07.160
 Um, the movement is really happening at that grassroots level and it's very diverse.

00:31:07.220 --> 00:31:08.780
 It's multifaceted.

00:31:08.780 --> 00:31:12.700
 It's really working at all these different entry points in the system.

00:31:13.340 --> 00:31:30.910
 And, and that's the food movement that I get inspired by and that I see and that I try to amplify and, um, uh, and, and showcase, uh, to, to, to really help disabuse people of that myth that the food movement, you know, are just rich people who care too much about the provenance of their wine.

00:31:31.310 --> 00:31:32.760
 Yeah, absolutely.

00:31:33.180 --> 00:31:41.580
 And I think, um, you know, anyone who does work in the food movement quickly runs into all the rest of those places, right?

00:31:41.700 --> 00:31:51.400
 So would you mind just walking, you know, naming a few of those examples for people who may not know what those grass movements are or some of the kinds of work that they do?

00:31:51.800 --> 00:31:52.020
 Yeah.

00:31:52.140 --> 00:31:59.620
 So one of the groups I've been working a lot with that I've just really come to respect so much is a group called the Food Chain Workers Alliance.

00:31:59.620 --> 00:32:16.080
 It started about five years ago and the idea behind it is that, you know, as we've been saying, food is a system, uh, there is a food chain that goes from, you know, from field to plate and there are workers at every point along that food chain.

00:32:16.080 --> 00:32:31.020
 And so, uh, the group, this group came together to say, you know, could we be stronger together if we brought together worker member organizations from the point of, uh, of the field to all the way to restaurant workers?

00:32:31.440 --> 00:32:35.520
 And could we, uh, come up with campaigns that we could work on together?

00:32:35.740 --> 00:32:38.740
 Could we share lessons learned across our experiences?

00:32:39.080 --> 00:32:43.120
 Could we help amplify our stories by working together?

00:32:43.820 --> 00:32:54.060
 And, uh, they've grown to, I think by latest count, they have 23 worker member organizations that are now part of the Alliance representing hundreds of thousands of members.

00:32:54.060 --> 00:32:57.920
 So that's a great example to me of one of those coalition groups.

00:32:59.120 --> 00:33:02.530
 Is that your project that you've been involved with called Voices of a Food Chain?

00:33:03.360 --> 00:33:05.220
 Is that, are those related to each other?

00:33:05.220 --> 00:33:07.040
 Yeah.

00:33:07.140 --> 00:33:19.630
 So Voices of the Food Chain is a project that Real Food Media, the group that I run, uh, created with Food Chain Workers Alliance with this idea that we really wanted to amplify the voices of workers.

00:33:19.950 --> 00:33:25.010
 So we partnered with the Alliance and with a wonderful organization called StoryCorps.

00:33:25.370 --> 00:33:29.120
 Uh, some of your listeners might be listeners of StoryCorps as well.

00:33:29.240 --> 00:33:35.160
 Really, uh, beautiful project that captures the voices of people all across the country.

00:33:35.160 --> 00:33:45.350
 And, uh, so I wanted to capture the voices of workers and we brought StoryCorps to an Alliance, uh, meeting and, and help those workers share their stories with each other.

00:33:45.350 --> 00:33:51.450
 And then created Voices of the Food Chain dot com to, to showcase those stories and have done other work through that project.

00:33:51.750 --> 00:33:57.730
 But it, it, to me, I feel like the food worker voice is, is one of the missing voices.

00:33:57.730 --> 00:33:59.510
 We don't hear enough from food workers.

00:33:59.810 --> 00:34:11.220
 And if we listen to their voices and heard their voices, I think we would learn so much more about what changes are needed to really create a more sustainable food system.

00:34:11.400 --> 00:34:15.570
 So I'm very passionate about getting their voices into, into the mainstream.

00:34:15.900 --> 00:34:24.260
 That's exciting for me because tomorrow I'm interviewing Saru Jayaraman about, um, her new book, Forked.

00:34:24.720 --> 00:34:24.900
 Great.

00:34:25.000 --> 00:34:40.980
 She, you know, she works with restaurant workers and I think a lot, um, for me, there's so much that stands out about this kind of choral representation of voices in, in a food system.

00:34:40.980 --> 00:34:41.420
 Right.

00:34:41.540 --> 00:34:51.620
 When you, I listened to just a few of those from Voices of the Food Chain and I thought about how many, how many of these people we actually know, right.

00:34:51.640 --> 00:34:53.320
 And interact with all the time.

00:34:53.320 --> 00:34:57.910
 And it kind of becomes impossible to see them as other in this process.

00:34:57.910 --> 00:34:58.820
 Right.

00:34:58.940 --> 00:35:05.180
 And you're talking about 20 million people who are workers in the food system in the United States alone.

00:35:05.900 --> 00:35:11.360
 And they are consistently the most underpaid and exploited workers in the economy.

00:35:11.940 --> 00:35:15.680
 Seven of the 10 lowest paying jobs in this country are in the food sector.

00:35:15.820 --> 00:35:21.600
 And as you'll hear from Saru, who is a founder of the Restaurant Opportunity Center, many of those are in the restaurant industry.

00:35:22.120 --> 00:35:41.320
 So you're talking about some of the most underpaid workers and so if you're, you're thinking about how do you address hunger in this country, how do you address poverty in this country, talking about paying food workers better and having their benefits restored is a really key part of that conversation.

00:35:41.480 --> 00:35:51.340
 And so food workers, as you've discussed a little bit, go all the way from farmers and people who work in fields to the people who serve your food at a restaurant.

00:35:52.130 --> 00:36:11.160
 Right. And what we're seeing in the American diet is this huge shift, a real radical shift in where we get our food, where we're eating it, what we're eating from 50, 60 years ago when most of the food we consumed, we consumed in our homes that we made ourselves to today.

00:36:11.160 --> 00:36:18.020
 We've just inched over the 50 percent mark where now the little bit more of the majority of our food we're eating out.

00:36:18.140 --> 00:36:34.370
 We're either eating out at quick serve restaurants or fast food restaurants or increasingly grocery stores are becoming semi-restaurants with pre, I'm sorry, grocery stores are becoming, you know, kind of these semi-restaurants where they have prepared foods that people are buying and eating.

00:36:34.830 --> 00:36:47.650
 So this is why you see the food sector as this really important sector to be addressing for both food access issues, sustainability issues, and worker welfare issues.

00:36:49.460 --> 00:36:55.540
 Yeah. I had no idea about that statistic. That's an enormous amount of food that's eaten out.

00:36:55.540 --> 00:37:15.000
 Yeah. And it's really, yeah, it changes our relationship to food and is one of the reasons why you see the spike in diet-related illnesses is because so much of that food we're eating outside of our homes has added sugars, added salt, has very high fat.

00:37:15.500 --> 00:37:32.770
 And so thinking about how do we improve the healthfulness of those foods and how do we also get people to still be eating in their homes and preparing their own foods, cooking their own foods.

00:37:33.920 --> 00:37:40.080
 So let's talk about quickly your myth-busting project and how that relates to that.

00:37:40.160 --> 00:37:42.560
 Does that address every aspect of the food system?

00:37:43.370 --> 00:37:56.350
 So foodmyths.org is our Food Mythbusters project, and we've done two myth-busting videos so far taking on two aspects of some of the biggest myths I see out there that are being pushed by the food industry.

00:37:57.170 --> 00:38:04.610
 And one is this myth that we need chemical agriculture and genetically engineered foods to feed the world.

00:38:05.170 --> 00:38:13.710
 It's a very persistent myth. I hear it all the time when I speak to audiences about sustainable food, organic farming.

00:38:13.710 --> 00:38:23.580
 One of the most frequent questions I hear is, but Anna, can we really feed the world using organic farming practices or using sustainable practices?

00:38:24.340 --> 00:38:44.210
 And it struck me that this question that comes up so often is the myth that is put out there by the public relations firms hired by some of the biggest agrochemical companies in the world and grain traders in the world and the companies that are benefiting from the status quo.

00:38:44.210 --> 00:38:57.870
 So I really wanted to take that myth on and create a video that helps people debunk that myth themselves and then be able to debunk it when they're talking to their cousins or their fellow students or their teachers.

00:38:58.530 --> 00:39:15.420
 Because if we can't upend that myth, I think we are so limited in what we're able to argue because no one, no one wants to feel that the kind of food system they're promoting would cause hunger in the world.

00:39:15.420 --> 00:39:31.820
 And so I find a lot of people are reticent to embrace organic farming as a solution, as a global solution, because they fear that there might be this ramification that it might cause hunger and nothing could be farther from the truth.

00:39:32.140 --> 00:39:40.640
 And there's a lot of reasons why it's a myth that it's a myth that it's a myth that is so easy to debunk.

00:39:41.080 --> 00:39:43.400
 And again, it's so important to do so.

00:39:45.860 --> 00:39:46.000
 Right.

00:39:46.120 --> 00:39:49.990
 And it comes from it's a big myth to debunk, though, right?

00:39:50.270 --> 00:39:51.190
 Oh, it's huge.

00:39:51.330 --> 00:39:54.750
 I mean, it's the myth my mom has been trying to debunk for four decades.

00:39:55.250 --> 00:40:00.150
 But the reason why it is so hard to debunk isn't because the evidence is on our side.

00:40:00.370 --> 00:40:09.690
 It's because there is such a profit to be made in the status quo.

00:40:09.830 --> 00:40:25.890
 There are such interests aligned with having us believe that we need this chemical arsenal in order to feed the world, that we need to be using these genetically engineered seeds to feed the world, that we need synthetic fertilizer to feed the world.

00:40:25.890 --> 00:40:40.290
 You're talking about billions and billions of dollars at stake, that there's a powerful vested interest in keeping us believing that we need these practices when the science increasingly shows.

00:40:40.430 --> 00:40:44.730
 And this is, again, people have been trying to up in this myth for decades.

00:40:44.730 --> 00:41:09.860
 And increasingly, there is scientific evidence that shows that farming solutions that help farmers get rid of their chemicals, get rid of those petrochemicals and the toxic chemicals used on the field, farming solutions that get farmers to build up their fertility with the ecology of the farm,

00:41:09.860 --> 00:41:24.740
 not with synthetic fertilizers that they have to buy and spend a lot of money on and that incredibly energy intensive to produce, that those are the solutions that not only create great productivity at the farm level, but they're also economically great for farmers.

00:41:24.740 --> 00:41:35.590
 Because you're not actually having to be dependent on buying chemicals or buying fertilizer or buying seeds.

00:41:36.010 --> 00:41:46.320
 A farmer, especially a cash-strapped farmer in the developing world, is able to resource its farm itself.

00:41:46.840 --> 00:41:48.520
 And that is powerful stuff.

00:41:48.520 --> 00:42:00.060
 And again, in the decades since my mother and her colleagues have been working to take on this myth, there's been just increasing evidence of the power of these sustainable solutions.

00:42:00.600 --> 00:42:06.200
 She just released a new edition of her book, World Hunger, 10 Myths.

00:42:06.200 --> 00:42:18.150
 And I highly recommend it as kind of the textbook if you're going to read one book about hunger in the world and how do we take it on and how do we solve it.

00:42:18.150 --> 00:42:34.350
 World Hunger, 10 Myths is the best resource out there and really goes into depth with some of the best peer-reviewed studies, the best research out there to really show the power of sustainable solutions and why we need to take this myth on.

00:42:36.130 --> 00:42:38.010
 Right. I'm so glad that you mentioned that.

00:42:38.130 --> 00:42:39.410
 That's a good resource for people.

00:42:40.120 --> 00:42:48.490
 So maybe let's finish up by having you tell me about Real Food Media and what the idea behind that is and what you're doing with that.

00:42:48.490 --> 00:43:00.880
 Real Food Media is a project I started several years ago with a coalition of groups working on food system change from a variety of entry points that we've been discussing today.

00:43:01.240 --> 00:43:06.320
 Groups that come at it from the environmental perspective or the worker justice perspective or the public health perspective.

00:43:07.020 --> 00:43:34.090
 And our idea behind Real Food Media was to create an initiative that would really help communicate the stories of solutions and communicate these myth-busting messages to really help promote the power of sustainable food and farming and to really help engage people with how they could be part of the movement, how they could be part of really shifting the food system toward more justice and more sustainability.

00:43:34.410 --> 00:43:38.120
 So we've done it through this work through a couple of different ways since we started.

00:43:38.120 --> 00:43:50.200
 We have an international short films competition called Real Food Films that showcases the best of the crop every year of contributed films.

00:43:50.340 --> 00:43:59.860
 We have 10 films every year that we promote as part of our pop-up film festivals that anybody in the world can host and show in their community.

00:44:00.120 --> 00:44:12.270
 We have about 80 films now in our film library all under four minutes on a range of topics that any educator can use, any community can use in workshops, in classrooms, in conferences.

00:44:12.950 --> 00:44:21.450
 So Real Food Films is our attempt to catalyze creative short films and then to share those films and distribute them widely through partners around the world.

00:44:22.320 --> 00:44:27.580
 And then we mentioned Food Mythbusters, that's our work to really take on big myths in the food system.

00:44:27.940 --> 00:44:33.060
 And our other work is these creative partnerships that we do.

00:44:33.220 --> 00:44:35.100
 So one was the Voices of the Food Chain.

00:44:35.480 --> 00:44:48.660
 We're also working on a new project with the partners at the Center for Good Food Purchasing to help pass the good food purchasing policy in cities across the country and helping them with their storytelling and communications.

00:44:50.090 --> 00:45:01.580
 And we do a lot of other public education, all, we hope, really part of helping to strengthen the food movement and helping inspire more people to get involved.

00:45:02.900 --> 00:45:04.240
 Wonderful. That's really important.

00:45:04.780 --> 00:45:06.340
 How can we follow along with that?

00:45:07.060 --> 00:45:08.010
 It's very easy.

00:45:08.170 --> 00:45:09.350
 You can go to Real Food Media.

00:45:10.010 --> 00:45:12.950
 We have lots of ways to follow our work there.

00:45:13.410 --> 00:45:15.460
 I'm on Twitter at Anna LaPay.

00:45:15.460 --> 00:45:20.480
 You can also check out our movies at realfoodfilms.org.

00:45:20.940 --> 00:45:24.680
 And if you want to host your own pop-up, we highly recommend it.

00:45:24.760 --> 00:45:25.820
 They're really fun to host.

00:45:25.960 --> 00:45:26.360
 They're free.

00:45:26.680 --> 00:45:29.420
 And you can go to realfoodfilms.org to find out more about that.

00:45:30.180 --> 00:45:30.460
 Wonderful.

00:45:31.080 --> 00:45:33.340
 Well, thanks so much for taking the time today.

00:45:33.420 --> 00:45:34.920
 It's been really wonderful to talk to you.

00:45:35.230 --> 00:45:36.330
 Oh, thank you so much.

00:45:36.370 --> 00:45:37.070
 It's been really fun.

